# Firestore Security Rules Conventions

## File Location

Security rules live in `databases/firestore.rules`. Index definitions in
`databases/firestore.indexes.json`.

## Rule Structure

Follow this top-level structure:

```
rules_version = '2';
service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    // Helper functions at the top
    // Then match blocks organized by collection hierarchy
  }
}
```

## Helper Functions

Define reusable helper functions at the top of the rules file:

```
function isSignedIn() {
  return request.auth != null;
}

function isOrgMember(orgId) {
  return isSignedIn() && request.auth.token.orgId == orgId;
}

function isOrgAdmin(orgId) {
  return isOrgMember(orgId) && request.auth.token.role == 'admin';
}

function isOwner(resource) {
  return isSignedIn() && resource.data.createdBy == request.auth.uid;
}

function isValidTimestamp(field) {
  return request.resource.data[field] is timestamp;
}
```

## Org-Scoping (Critical)

Every collection under `orgs/{orgId}` must enforce org membership. This is the
most important security invariant in the system:

```
match /orgs/{orgId} {
  allow read: if isOrgMember(orgId);
  allow update: if isOrgAdmin(orgId);
  // No create/delete from client -- handled by Cloud Functions

  match /projects/{projectId} {
    allow read: if isOrgMember(orgId);
    allow create: if isOrgMember(orgId)
      && request.resource.data.createdBy == request.auth.uid
      && isValidTimestamp('createdAt');
    allow update: if isOrgMember(orgId);
    allow delete: if isOrgAdmin(orgId) || isOwner(resource);
  }

  match /signals/{signalId} {
    allow read: if isOrgMember(orgId);
    allow create: if isOrgMember(orgId)
      && request.resource.data.createdBy == request.auth.uid;
    allow update: if isOrgMember(orgId);
    allow delete: if isOrgAdmin(orgId);
  }

  match /members/{userId} {
    allow read: if isOrgMember(orgId);
    allow write: if isOrgAdmin(orgId);
  }
}
```

## User Profile Rules

User profiles are cross-org and self-managed:

```
match /users/{userId} {
  allow read: if isSignedIn();
  allow create: if request.auth.uid == userId;
  allow update: if request.auth.uid == userId;
  allow delete: if false; // Handled by Cloud Functions
}
```

## Write Validation

Validate document structure on writes:

```
allow create: if isOrgMember(orgId)
  && request.resource.data.keys().hasAll(['title', 'createdAt', 'createdBy'])
  && request.resource.data.title is string
  && request.resource.data.title.size() > 0
  && request.resource.data.title.size() <= 200
  && request.resource.data.createdBy == request.auth.uid;
```

## Rules to Follow

1. **Default deny**: if no rule matches, access is denied. Never add a blanket `allow read, write: if true`.
2. **Org-scoping on every subcollection**: every `match` under `orgs/{orgId}` must check `isOrgMember(orgId)`.
3. **No client deletes on critical data**: orgs and users are deleted only via Cloud Functions with Admin SDK.
4. **Validate required fields**: `create` rules should check that required fields exist and have correct types.
5. **Prevent privilege escalation**: users cannot set their own `role` or `orgId` claims. Only Cloud Functions can modify custom claims.
6. **Size limits**: validate string lengths to prevent abuse (`title.size() <= 200`).
7. **createdBy immutability**: on `update`, ensure `createdBy` cannot be changed: `request.resource.data.createdBy == resource.data.createdBy`.

## Testing Rules

Always test rules before deploying:

```bash
npm run test:rules
```

Tests live in `tests/rules/` and use `@firebase/rules-unit-testing`:

```typescript
import { assertSucceeds, assertFails } from "@firebase/rules-unit-testing";

it("allows org members to read signals", async () => {
  const db = getFirestoreForUser({ uid: "user1", orgId: "org1", role: "member" });
  await assertSucceeds(getDoc(doc(db, "orgs/org1/signals/signal1")));
});

it("denies cross-org reads", async () => {
  const db = getFirestoreForUser({ uid: "user1", orgId: "org1", role: "member" });
  await assertFails(getDoc(doc(db, "orgs/org2/signals/signal1")));
});
```

## Deployment

Deploy rules separately from functions:

```bash
npm run deploy:rules  # Deploys firestore.rules + firestore.indexes.json
```

Never deploy rules and functions in the same command to isolate failures.
